hi there Steve Kaufman here today was a  heavy Korean learning day I am preparing  for my trip I'm going to Boston tomorrow  to the American Conference of teachers  of foreign languages convention there  and so I went through a lot of Korean  texts looking words up in the dictionary  so that there socked in as saved links  on my iPad and my iPhone so that I can  study them offline I went through  basically just saving the blue words now  I've got a bunch of yellow words I'm  going to read through the text while I'm  on the airplane and I got my handy  Korean history textbook with the Chinese  characters but today I want a ramble a  bit because I want to refer to there's  reference here in the comments to a blog  posed by a person who essentially  attacked all the principles of input  based learning stephen krashen and so  forth so I want to ramble a bit in  response to this blog post since I'm  going to Boston I'm gonna have a blast  there with all these teachers and people  who are interested in languages and I am  a supported receiving crash who's gonna  be there by the way I'm looking forward  to meeting him there but here is what  this person had to say this person's  name is John Franco Conde a co-author of  the language teacher toolkit winner of  the 2015 best resource contributor award  blah blah blah and he is a PhD in  applied linguistics MA in teaching to  the foreign language and Maine  literature English Center and Sorenson  his point is that there are five mixed  messages which have severely damaged  modern language education and he  basically blames stephen krashen and the  first of these is the idea that we  shouldn't teach grammar because we  should learn like children and children  learn subconsciously well it's an  oversimplification of course we don't  learn like children because we have our  life experience and our vocabulary in  our own language which the children  don't have but there are many things  that the children do that we can imitate  and that is exposing ourselves to a lot  of  content and it doesn't it isn't that we  shouldn't teach any grammar but I I  believe that we shouldn't expect that  because we teach grammar people are  gonna get it right a lot of grammar is  very immune to explanation correctness  over the zone I know this all the time  we see we see who people who can't get  the third person singular the present  tense right after 10 or 15 years of  grammar instruction he do he go she go  I always mentioned Chinese people who  after 10 15 years or longer are still  saying my husband she it just takes a  while for these things to sink in and  the more sort of framework reference  framework we have the more the grammar  instruction is relevant so I think that  number one point crashing emphasizes  meaningful input as a major contributor  to language acquisition  I think crashing is right and our friend  John Franco is wrong the second point he  says is don't use the first language in  the classroom I don't need to go through  all of it but I agree with you I think  where necessary use the first language  in the classroom if you're teaching  Spanish person doesn't understand what  you're saying use some English stuff  doesn't even answer in English and also  dictionaries I always prefer a bilingual  dictionary as opposed to one that's the  monolingual dictionary where the  dictionary definition contains a lot of  words that again I don't understand so  that's true I'll go for that  the third point he says is that a myth  that's damaging right and his point is  that somehow it's since the seventies  since learner based instruction and  crashing that everything has gone to  hell well I studied language in the  language early in the 50s people didn't  learn in Montreal all the people in  English school system didn't learn to  speak French any more than they do now  and what's more language instruction is  still heavily grammar focused that  hasn't changed us the most common  complaint that I get from people at link  or on my youtube channel is so but then  he says another myth era is that it's  not important for students to understand  all they read or hear well there's all  kinds of research  and I mentioned the professor Robert  Bjork who points out that we need we  shouldn't try and force ourselves to  understand something we should we should  interleave we should do a little bit of  this a little bit of that and go back to  the other and if we learn and forget  learn and forget we're gonna learn  better than if we try to force ourselves  to learn something to master it as he  says to master 90 95 percent of it  I think the main thing is to do things  that you find interesting stimulating  and and move on as long as you're  motivated to try and understand a bit  more stay with it  but once you find it boring or  uninteresting move on to something else  so it's not necessary he says you have  to understand 99.5% of the text before  I'm moving on I don't believe that but I  can see that in a classroom context that  might be something to strive for then he  says another myth is that teachers must  be tolerant of mistakes I think the main  thing again is to get people  communicating some mistakes are not  gonna correct themselves no matter how  much you correct people I have done it  you know tutoring people via Skype you  correct them they come right back with  them same mistake same with me I make a  mistake tutor corrects me sends it you  know on my link conversation report and  then the same mistake is corrected in  the following conversation it takes a  long time and the bulk of these mistakes  we correct them ourselves after enough  exposure so teachers not necessarily be  tolerant of mistakes but it's not a big  deal to make mistakes so that's also and  he says that if we if the teachers  tolerate mistakes the mistakes will be  fossilized will be ingrained in the  learner but what does that mean if the  bulk of what the learner is doing is  listening and reading if I'm listening  to stuff if I'm reading how am I in  graining errors if you don't demand of  me that I produce the language all the  time which i think is unnecessary at an  early stage  I'm not going to ingrain any errors but  I might just be enjoying what I'm  listening to and reading and the final  one he says is that another myth is of  pronunciation is not important it's only  important to be understood well again  it's like this big issue of grammar it's  not  no grammar but is how much emphasis to  put our driver so how much emphasis  should you put on pronunciation should  you make people self-conscious about  their pronunciation particularly early  on I find that it takes a long time for  me to get used to the intonation of the  language the pronunciation and sometimes  some things that I don't notice in the  pronunciation at an early stage I start  to notice later on so when I can hear  things better and I notice things better  I believe I have a better chance of  pronouncing them better  so again if the teacher is forcing  people to speak in the classroom wants  them to speak correctly he's irritated  by their mistakes he's irritated by  their poor pronunciation maybe that's an  issue but if you can get people to learn  and hear again his assumption is that  kids won't read kids won't just listen  but my grandchildren who went to French  immersion they had to read on history  and chemistry and physics and math in  French and they did it so you can get  kids to do that and maybe instead of  questioning them on every detail and  making sure they correctly answer  questions about what happened in a  particular bit of reading that they did  if you simply said again if they're  using link make sure they create links  on every page or they're looking words  up but also write a bit about your  impression of what you read to show the  derivative essentially and use some of  the words that you learned and we won't  mark you down for grammar mistakes and  we won't won't mark you down for missing  pressures about what happened in the  story we just want you to use the  language and show that you listen and  read and you enjoyed it and maybe your  interpretation is different than the you  know call it the correct interpretation  but you enjoyed that engagement with the  language enough so in other words I  think that it all boils down to what  this lady who I hope I will see in  Boston and whom I met in San Diego the  last time I attended one of these  conferences dr. Mary Ann Lyman Hagar who  at that time  the head of the language acquisition  Resource Center at Sandia San Diego  State University and who said there are  only three things that mattered in  language learning the attitude of the  learner the time spent with the language  and developing the ability to notice and  I think that developing the ability to  notice comes from spending the time  comes from having the right attitude and  those are the only thing that only  things that matter so essentially I  reject this criticism of crashing  however I do recognize that those people  who like to go at language learning in  some other way with an emphasis on Anki  or an emphasis on grammar if that's what  they enjoy doing if that's what gets  them to spend time with the language if  that's what turns their crank therefore  gives them a positive attitude by all  means go for it anyway I'm going for it  I'm going to Boston tomorrow morning and  bye for now
 

